# Blocks `git commit -m "…`backtick`…"` — the shell eats the backticked word.
#
# NOT once-per-session. Like 10-jest-pipe this is a hard stop on a mechanical
# mistake, not a nudge toward a skill; making it spendable by doing it twice
# would defeat it.
#
# Why: inside DOUBLE quotes bash treats `…` as command substitution. Writing a
# commit body with markdown code spans — which is the natural way to name a
# symbol — silently deletes the word and runs it as a command:
#
#     git commit -m "the `why` field states the basis"
#       → stderr: command not found: why
#       → commit message: "the  field states the basis"
#
# The commit still succeeds, so nothing fails and the damage is only visible if
# you re-read the message afterwards. Observed repeatedly; the 2026-08-24
# occurrence lost a word from a pushed commit, and amending a pushed develop
# commit costs a force-push that is not worth one word.
#
# Single quotes are safe (no expansion), so this fires only on the double-quoted
# form. `$(…)` is deliberately NOT matched: unlike a markdown code span it is
# occasionally intentional in a commit message (a version string), and a guard
# that cries wolf gets switched off.
#
# KNOWN FALSE POSITIVE, accepted: the rule sees a whole Bash command, so it also
# fires on a command that merely CONTAINS all three tokens without being a commit
# — a python/bash heredoc that writes ABOUT commit messages, for instance. That
# happened twice while writing this rule's own tests. The escape is to make such
# edits with the Write/Edit tools rather than a shell heredoc, which is better
# practice anyway. Narrowing it to "backtick appears after the -m" is not worth
# the regex fragility for a case this rare.
rule_id=commit-backtick
rule_once=0

rule_match() {
    [ "$1" = "Bash" ] || return 1
    # All three must hold: a git commit, a double-quoted -m, and a backtick.
    printf '%s' "$2" | grep -q 'git commit' || return 1
    printf '%s' "$2" | grep -q -- '-m[[:space:]]*"' || return 1
    printf '%s' "$2" | grep -q '`'
}

rule_message() {
    cat <<'MSG'
Blocked: backticks inside a double-quoted `git commit -m "…"` are command
substitution. Bash runs the backticked word and DELETES it from the message —
the commit still succeeds, so the damage is silent.

Use a quoted heredoc instead. The quoted delimiter disables every expansion,
so backticks, $, and quotes all survive verbatim:

    git commit -F - <<'MSG'
    feat(scope): subject line

    Body may contain `backticks`, $vars and "quotes" safely.
    MSG

(Single-quoted -m '…' is also safe, but breaks on apostrophes — prefer the heredoc.)
MSG
}
